text:
maria: Quick question — our SDKs currently return a generic ApiError for pretty much everything. Can we move to typed exceptions so callers can do `except RateLimitError` instead of parsing message text?

jonathan: We talked about this last quarter but never landed a cross-SDK contract. Current behaviour: 429/5xx/401/403 all come back as ApiError with a status field and a long message. Not great for ergonomics.

priya: Strong +1 for typed errors. We should also surface `retry_after` (from Retry-After header) and a `should_retry` boolean so callers don't have to implement heuristics.

sam: Also important: distinguish 401 (expired/invalid token) vs 403 (permission denied). Teams keep rebinding credentials on 403s because they treat it as auth failure. That causes noisy support tickets.

kevin: Proposal sketch: a small hierarchy rooted at `RedwoodError` (or `RedwoodApiError`) with concrete subtypes: `RateLimitError`, `TransientServerError` (5xx), `AuthExpiredError` (401 + token expired), `PermissionDeniedError` (403), `BadRequestError` (4xx other), and `StreamError` for mid-stream failures. Each error exposes: `status_code`, `message`, optional `retry_after` (seconds), `is_transient` boolean, and `cause` (raw payload).

maria: Should we keep `status_code` on the exception or hide it and force callers to use types? I'm leaning to keeping it for observability but encouraging typed checks.

jonathan: For JS/TS we prefer class-based errors with typed props: `error.retryAfter?: number; error.isTransient: boolean;`. For Python keep attrs the same. For Go return typed error interfaces with methods: `RetryAfter() (time.Duration, bool)` and `IsTransient() bool`.

priya: Draft catch examples so we can add to docs. Example Python pattern:

```python
try:
    resp = client.generate(prompt)
except client.errors.RateLimitError as e:
    sleep(e.retry_after or backoff.next())
except client.errors.AuthExpiredError:
    refresh_tokens()
except client.errors.PermissionDeniedError:
    alert_oncall()
```

and JS/TS:

```js
try {
  const resp = await client.generate(...)
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof client.errors.RateLimitError) {
    await wait(err.retryAfter ?? backoff.next())
  } else if (err instanceof client.errors.AuthExpiredError) {
    await auth.refresh()
  } else {
    throw err
  }
}
```

sam: Streaming special case: if an error happens mid-stream we should emit a terminal event that includes the typed error (not just a status code). Consumers of the stream can then decide to retry or show a friendly message.

kevin: Agreed. `StreamError` should include `offset`/`tokens_emitted` and `cause` so we can do partial-resume strategies later.

maria: Migration plan thoughts: 1) introduce new types behind minor version flag, 2) keep ApiError for a deprecation window, 3) add runtime feature flag to toggle strict typed errors for internal testing, 4) docs + quickstart snippets. Also add linters in SDK repos to ensure parity.

jonathan: I'll draft an RFC with concrete API shapes and suggested wording for error messages. I'll include a compatibility table mapping current status codes -> new types.

priya: Can you include specific wording for the user-facing message for RateLimitError? teams like seeing "Request quota exhausted — retry after Xs" instead of opaque JSON.

jonathan: Yep. I'll put examples.

ci-bot: Deploy bot: new PR: jonathan/typed-errors-draft ready for review -> https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/812 :rocket:

sam: One more ask: add end-to-end tests that simulate Retry-After header present/absent and assert `.retry_after` value and `.is_transient`. Those tests should run in CI.

kevin: And add a small integration test for streaming where server returns an HTTP 500 mid-stream and SDK surfaces `StreamError`.

maria: Fantastic. Jonathan, can you tag the SDK leads in the RFC when it's ready? I can help with the migration notes and release checklist.

jonathan: Will do, aiming for a draft by EOD tomorrow. Ping me if you have specific examples to include.

priya: :thumbsup:

maria: Thanks all — this will really reduce friction for customers and make our support data cleaner.
